## 12. Summary: Key Decisions & Tradeoffs

| Decision | Rationale | Tradeoff |
|----------|-----------|----------|
| **Worker thread sandbox** | Isolate CPU/memory per plugin; no cross-plugin crashes | Slightly higher memory overhead (~50MB per sandbox) |
| **Manifest-based configuration** | Declarative, enables host to pre-validate capabilities | Less flexible than programmatic config; requires schema updates for new capabilities |
| **Semantic versioning + deprecation** | Clear migration path; predictable breaking changes | Requires discipline in v1.x to avoid breaking changes |
| **Capability grants** | Fine-grained permission model; supports RBAC | More complex for simple "trust all" deployments |
| **Hook-based lifecycle** | Simple, predictable state machine; clear error handling | Requires plugins to cleanup state (no implicit release) |
| **PluginAPI versioning independent of host** | Plugins don't break on host upgrades; N versions supported simultaneously | Parallel maintenance of multiple API versions |

---

This architecture is production-ready and designed to support five teams building plugins concurrently. The manifest schema is explicit enough to prevent ambiguity, the sandbox provides real isolation guarantees, and the versioned API contract ensures teams can develop without blocking each other on breaking changes.
