## 7. Error Handling & Recovery

Plugins fail; the architecture ensures failures are isolated and recoverable.

### 7.1 Error Categories

```typescript
abstract class PluginError extends Error {
  pluginId: string;
  timestamp: ISO8601DateTime;
  context: ErrorContext;
  recoverable: boolean;
}

class PermissionDenied extends PluginError {
  permission: Permission;
  recoverable = false; // Needs admin re-grant
}

class Timeout extends PluginError {
  operationMs: number;
  limitMs: number;
  recoverable = true; // Can retry with higher limit
}

class OutOfMemory extends PluginError {
  usedMb: number;
  limitMb: number;
  recoverable = true; // Can reload to reset
}

class DependencyMissing extends PluginError {
  dependency: string;
  version: string;
  recoverable = false; // Requires re-installation
}

class APIVersionMismatch extends PluginError {
  requestedVersion: string;
  availableVersions: string[];
  recoverable = false; // Plugin incompatible with host
}

class CrashError extends PluginError {
  exitCode: number;
  stderr: string;
  recoverable = true; // Can reload
}

interface ErrorContext {
  operation: string;
  requestId: string;
  userId?: string;
  timestamp: ISO8601DateTime;
  stackTrace?: string;
}
```

### 7.2 Recovery Strategies

| Error | Auto-Recovery | Admin Action |
|-------|---------------|--------------|
| Timeout | Retry with backoff (3x) | Increase `timeoutPermission` |
| OOM | Reload plugin | Increase `memoryPermission` or optimize plugin |
| Crash | Reload (1 per hour) | Review error logs; update plugin |
| PermissionDenied | None (blocked) | Grant permission in manifest |
| APIVersionMismatch | None (blocked) | Require plugin upgrade |
| DependencyMissing | None (blocked) | Re-run plugin installation |

### 7.3 Failure Isolation

A failed plugin does NOT:
- Crash the host application
- Block other plugins
- Leak memory or file descriptors
- Leave dangling connections

When a plugin fails:
1. Error is caught at the API boundary
2. Plugin state set to `FAILED` with error details
3. User-facing request returns: `{ error: "Plugin X unavailable", details: { ... } }`
4. Logs captured; admin notified if enabled
5. Subsequent calls to failed plugin are fast-failed (no retry)

