## Executive Summary

This document specifies the complete plugin architecture for the reporting tool. It enables safe third-party extension through a versioned API contract, sandboxed execution, lifecycle management, and a declarative manifest schema. The system supports dependency injection, resource isolation, and graceful degradation when plugins fail.

---

## 1. Architecture Overview

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                    Reporting Tool Core                       │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│  Plugin Registry    │  Lifecycle Manager  │  Sandboxbox      │
│  (Discovery,Load)   │  (Hooks, State)     │  (Workers, IPC)  │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│           Plugin API Contract (Versioned)                     │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Plugin 1          │ Plugin 2           │ Plugin N            │
│ (Isolated)        │ (Isolated)         │ (Isolated)          │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

**Core Principles:**
- **Isolation**: Plugins run in separate execution contexts (worker threads/processes)
- **Declarative**: Manifest-driven configuration; no runtime discovery magic
- **Versioned**: API versioning allows graceful evolution and deprecation
- **Fault-tolerant**: Plugin failures do not crash the core or other plugins
- **Observable**: Plugins are introspectable; core can monitor health and resource usage

