## 4. Plugin Lifecycle Hooks

Plugins participate in a managed lifecycle with well-defined hook points. All hooks are async and support error handling with rollback semantics.

### 4.1 Hook Types

```typescript
interface LifecycleHooks {
  // Load: Plugin is being initialized
  // Called once when plugin first loads
  // Errors here prevent the plugin from starting
  load?: (context: PluginContext) => Promise<void>;

  // Unload: Plugin is being shut down
  // Called before terminating the plugin
  // Errors are logged but don't prevent unload
  unload?: (context: PluginContext) => Promise<void>;

  // Reload: Plugin is being reloaded (unload + load)
  // Called after unload, before load
  // Useful for migration/cleanup
  reload?: (context: PluginContext) => Promise<void>;

  // Pre-report: Before report generation
  // Called for each report that uses this plugin's capabilities
  // Can modify report config or cancel report
  'pre-report'?: (
    context: PluginContext,
    report: ReportDefinition
  ) => Promise<void | ModificationResult>;

  // Post-report: After successful report generation
  // Called after report is rendered/exported
  // Can enrich report with metadata
  'post-report'?: (
    context: PluginContext,
    report: GeneratedReport,
    result: ReportResult
  ) => Promise<void>;
}

interface PluginContext {
  // Plugin identification
  pluginId: string;
  manifest: PluginManifest;
  config: PluginConfig;

  // API access (versioned)
  api: ReportingAPIv1_2;

  // State management
  state: PluginState;

  // Logging
  logger: Logger;

  // Resource access (permission-gated)
  resourceManager: ResourceManager;
}
```

### 4.2 Hook Execution Model

```typescript
interface HookExecutor {
  // Execute a lifecycle hook with timeout and error handling
  execute<T>(
    pluginId: string,
    hookName: string,
    fn: (context: PluginContext) => Promise<T>,
    options?: {
      timeoutMs?: number;  // Default: 30s
      rollback?: () => Promise<void>;  // Called on error
      critical?: boolean;  // If true, error aborts; else logged
    }
  ): Promise<HookResult<T>>;
}

interface HookResult<T> {
  success: boolean;
  data?: T;
  error?: Error;
  duration: number;
  workerPid?: number;
}
```

### 4.3 Hook Execution Flow

```
┌─ Plugin.load() Hook
│  │
│  ├─ Timeout: 30s (configurable)
│  ├─ Error: Prevent plugin load, add to disabled
│  └─ Success: Register plugin as available
│
├─ Plugin.reload() Hook (optional)
│  │
│  ├─ Timeout: 60s
│  ├─ Error: Log warning, continue
│  └─ Purpose: Handle migrations, config changes
│
├─ Report.pre-report() Hooks
│  │
│  ├─ Called for all plugins with matching capability
│  ├─ Sequential execution (topologically ordered)
│  ├─ Timeout: 10s per plugin
│  ├─ Error: Skip plugin, log, continue (non-critical)
│  │    or abort report if marked critical
│  └─ Output: Modified report config or skip signal
│
├─ [Report Generation]
│
├─ Report.post-report() Hooks
│  │
│  ├─ Called for all plugins with matching capability
│  ├─ Sequential execution (reverse order of pre-report)
│  ├─ Timeout: 10s per plugin
│  ├─ Error: Log, do not affect generated report
│  └─ Output: Metadata to attach to report
│
└─ Plugin.unload() Hook
   │
   ├─ Timeout: 5s
   ├─ Error: Force cleanup after timeout
   └─ Purpose: Resource cleanup, persistence
```

