## 7. Plugin Configuration & State

Plugins have two kinds of state: configuration (provided by admin) and runtime state (maintained by plugin).

### 7.1 Configuration Management

```typescript
interface PluginConfig {
  // User-provided values matching manifest.config.schema
  [key: string]: unknown;
}

interface ConfigManager {
  // Get config value with type safety
  get<T = unknown>(key: string, defaultValue?: T): T;

  // Update config (persisted)
  set<T>(key: string, value: T): Promise<void>;

  // Watch for changes
  onChange<T>(
    key: string,
    callback: (newValue: T, oldValue: T) => void
  ): () => void; // Returns unsubscribe function

  // Validate against schema
  validate(): ValidationResult;
}
```

### 7.2 Plugin State Management

```typescript
interface PluginState {
  // Opaque storage for plugin-specific state
  // Persisted across reloads
  get(key: string): unknown;
  set(key: string, value: unknown): void;
  delete(key: string): void;
  clear(): void;

  // Export/import for migration
  export(): Record<string, unknown>;
  import(data: Record<string, unknown>): Promise<void>;
}

// State is persisted in:
// - File system: ${pluginStorageDir}/${pluginId}.state.json
// - Database: plugins_state table (for distributed systems)
// Serialization: JSON (plugins must serialize custom types)
