## 8. Error Handling & Recovery

Plugin failures must not cascade to the core system or other plugins.

### 8.1 Error Classification

```typescript
enum ErrorSeverity {
  // Plugin misconfiguration, skip silently
  WARNING = 'warning',

  // Plugin failed on this operation, fall back
  RECOVERABLE = 'recoverable',

  // Plugin is broken, disable it
  CRITICAL = 'critical',

  // System error (not plugin's fault)
  SYSTEM = 'system',
}

interface PluginError extends Error {
  severity: ErrorSeverity;
  pluginId: string;
  hookName?: string;
  timestamp: Date;
  context?: Record<string, unknown>;
  suggestedAction?: 'retry' | 'disable' | 'reload' | 'escalate';
}
```

### 8.2 Error Handling Strategies by Hook

| Hook | On Error | Action |
|------|----------|--------|
| `load` | Critical | Prevent plugin load, disable |
| `unload` | Warning | Force cleanup after timeout |
| `pre-report` | Recoverable | Skip plugin, continue report |
| `post-report` | Warning | Log only, don't affect report |
| `render chart` | Recoverable | Show error placeholder, continue |

### 8.3 Recovery Mechanisms

```typescript
interface ErrorRecovery {
  // Automatic retry with backoff
  async retry<T>(
    fn: () => Promise<T>,
    maxAttempts: number = 3,
    baseDelayMs: number = 100
  ): Promise<T>;

  // Graceful degradation
  fallback<T>(
    primary: () => Promise<T>,
    secondary: () => Promise<T>
  ): Promise<T>;

  // Circuit breaker for flaky plugins
  withCircuitBreaker(
    pluginId: string,
    fn: () => Promise<unknown>
  ): Promise<unknown>;
}
```

