## Migration & Compatibility

### Upgrade Path: API v1 → v2

Plugins targeting v1.0 API automatically get an adapter:

```typescript
class APIv1Adapter implements HostAPI {
  constructor(private v2API: HostAPIv2) {}

  get query() {
    return {
      execute: async (sql: string) => {
        // v1 didn't have caching, so ignore
        return this.v2API.query.execute(sql, { cache: false });
      },
      executePrepared: (statement: string, params: any[]) => {
        // v2 combines these into one method
        return this.v2API.query.execute(`${statement}?`, {
          params,
          cache: false,
        });
      },
    };
  }

  get report() {
    return {
      getReport: (id: string) => this.v2API.report.getReport(id),
      listReports: (filter?: any) => this.v2API.report.listReports(filter),
      getReportMetadata: (id: string) =>
        this.v2API.report.getReportMetadata(id),
      // Hide v2-only methods (write operations)
    };
  }
  // ... other API methods
}
```

### Deprecation Warnings

When a v1 plugin loads on host with v2 API:

```
WARN: Plugin 'analytics-plugin' uses deprecated API v1.0
  Alternative: Upgrade manifest.apiVersion to "2.0"
  Migration guide: https://docs.example.com/plugin-migration-guide
  Will be unsupported in: host v4.0.0
```

### Versioning Rules

**Never violate these:**
- ❌ Add required fields to manifest
- ❌ Remove capabilities without major version bump
- ❌ Change capability semantics within same major version
- ✅ Add optional fields to manifest
- ✅ Add new capabilities in minor versions
- ✅ Extend interfaces with new methods (backward compatible)

