## Sandboxed Execution

Plugins are untrusted. Sandbox isolates them, enforces limits, and mediates API access.

### Isolation Tiers

```
Tier 1: Process Isolation
  • Separate worker process/thread
  • OS-level resource limits (cgroups, job objects)
  • Complete memory isolation
  • Use: Untrusted plugins, high-risk code

Tier 2: VM Context Isolation (Recommended)
  • Shared process, separate V8 contexts
  • Proxy-based API access (revocable)
  • Memory limits via GC pressure
  • Use: Typical plugins, trusted partners

Tier 3: In-Process
  • Shared context, direct API access
  • No resource isolation
  • Use: Internal/built-in plugins only
```

### Sandbox Configuration

```typescript
interface SandboxConfig {
  isolationLevel: 1 | 2 | 3

  // Resource limits
  cpuLimit: number                 // ms per window
  cpuWindow: number                // e.g., 1000ms
  executionTimeout: number         // max ms per call
  memoryLimit: number              // MB

  // File system
  fileSystemAccess: {
    mode: "none" | "readonly" | "temp" | "full"
    maxFileSize: number
    maxTotalSize: number
  }

  // Network
  networkAccess: {
    mode: "none" | "internal" | "restricted" | "full"
    whitelist?: string[]
    blacklist?: string[]
    timeout: number
    rateLimit: number
  }

  // API access
  apiAccess: {
    whitelist: string[]            // Allowed methods
    rateLimits: Record<string, number>  // method → calls/sec
  }
}
```

### Enforcement Strategies

For **Node.js (Tier 2 default)**:
- Worker threads with memory limits
- Timeout enforcement via Promise.race
- Rate limiting via call counter
- API access via Proxy traps

For **Tier 1 (High security)**:
- Separate process with fork()
- OS-level cgroups (Linux) or job objects (Windows)
- Resource watcher for metrics

