}

Plugin Discovery Process:
  1. Scan local plugin directory for manifest.json
  2. Query remote registry API with authentication
  3. Scan git repositories for version tags
  4. Aggregate results from all sources
  5. Resolve dependencies recursively
  6. Detect version conflicts
  7. Build dependency graph
  8. Validate all manifests against schema
  9. Perform security scanning
  10. Return catalog of available plugins

================================================================================
4. LIFECYCLE MANAGEMENT
================================================================================

State Machine:

UNINSTALLED
    | install()
    v
INSTALLED
    | load()
    v
LOADING (onLoad hook executing)
    | [success within timeout]
    v
LOADED
    +---> [enable()]
    |     v
    |   ENABLING (onEnable hook executing)
    |     v
    +---> ENABLED
    |     | [disable()]
    |     v
    |   DISABLING (onDisable hook executing)
    |     v
    |   DISABLED ---+
    |              | [enable()]
    |              v
    +----------> ENABLING
    |
    | [unload()]
    v
UNLOADING (onUnload hook executing)
    v
UNINSTALLED

Error Handling in State Machine:
  - If manifest invalid: stay in UNINSTALLED, report error
  - If dependency unmet: stay in INSTALLED, report error
  - If load timeout: transition to LOADED with error flag
  - If load fails: transition to LOADED with error flag
  - If enable fails: rollback to LOADED (disable first)
  - If critical hook fails: mark plugin as ERROR
  - Recovery attempts trigger state machine transitions

Lifecycle Hooks:

onLoad(context: PluginContext): Promise<void>
  - Called when transitioning LOADING -> LOADED
  - Timeout: manifest.spec.hooks.load.timeout
  - Critical: If critical=true and fails, plugin enters ERROR
  - Rollback: On timeout/failure, cleanup sandbox
  - Use case: Initialize resources, connect to services

onEnable(context: PluginContext): Promise<void>
  - Called when transitioning ENABLING -> ENABLED
  - Timeout: manifest.spec.hooks.enable.timeout
  - Critical: If critical=true and fails, plugin stays DISABLED
  - Rollback: On failure, plugin transitions back to DISABLED
  - Use case: Start background tasks, begin request processing

onDisable(context: PluginContext): Promise<void>
  - Called when transitioning DISABLING -> DISABLED
  - Timeout: manifest.spec.hooks.disable.timeout
  - Rollback: On failure, log error but continue (best effort)
  - Use case: Pause request processing, flush pending work

onUnload(context: PluginContext): Promise<void>
  - Called when transitioning UNLOADING -> UNINSTALLED
  - Timeout: manifest.spec.hooks.unload.timeout
  - Rollback: On failure, log error but continue
  - Use case: Persist state, close connections, cleanup

onError(error: PluginError): Promise<void>
  - Called when error occurs at any lifecycle stage
  - Not part of state transition
  - For plugin-specific error handling logic
  - Must not throw

PluginContext Interface:

interface PluginContext {
  // Identity
  pluginId: string
  version: string
  namespace: string

  // Configuration and State
  config: Record<string, unknown>
  store: PluginStore               // Persistent state
  storage: PluginStorage           // Temporary state

  // Communication
  logger: Logger                   // Structured logging
  emit: (event: string, data: any) => void
  on: (event: string, handler: (data: any) => void) => void
  once: (event: string) => Promise<any>

  // Host API Access
  api: HostAPI                     // Capabilities-controlled

  // Observability
  metrics: MetricsContext
  traceId: string
  correlationId: string
}

Hook Execution Guarantees:

  1. Sequential Execution
     - Only one hook executes at a time per plugin
     - Dependent plugins wait for dependencies to complete
     - System waits for all critical hooks

  2. Timeout Enforcement
     - Timeout is hard deadline (Worker thread termination)
     - Timeout < manifest timeout uses manifest value
     - Exceeded timeout reported as PluginError

  3. Error Handling
     - Hook exception caught, logged, reported
     - onError hook called with PluginError
     - State transition aborted, plugin marked ERROR if critical

  4. Resource Cleanup
     - If hook times out, sandbox forcibly terminated
     - All resources released
     - Plugin can be reloaded

================================================================================
5. SANDBOXED EXECUTION BOUNDARY
================================================================================

Three Isolation Levels (configurable per plugin):

STRICT (Default - Most Secure)
  Execution:
    - Worker Thread with separate V8 isolate
    - No shared memory with host
    - Message passing IPC only
    - CPU/memory limits enforced by Node.js

  Restrictions:
    - Memory: 256MB limit (configurable per plugin)
    - Network: Explicit allowlist (domains, ports)
    - Filesystem: Complete access denial
    - Processes: Cannot spawn child processes
    - Globals: Restricted global scope (no process, fs, os)
    - Modules: Whitelist only safe modules

  Use Case:
    - Untrusted third-party plugins
    - Production environments
    - Plugins with high risk level

MODERATE (Medium Security/Performance)
  Execution:
    - Worker Thread with separate V8 isolate
    - Limited filesystem read access
    - Network less restricted
    - Performance-optimized resource limits

  Restrictions:
    - Memory: 512MB limit
    - Filesystem: Read-only access to designated paths
    - Network: Broader allowlist, fewer restrictions
    - Still no child process spawning
    - Still no global scope modification

  Use Case:
    - Trusted vendor plugins
    - Internal plugins from known teams
    - Staging/development environments

PERMISSIVE (Minimum Security)
  Execution:
    - Same-process execution (VM2-like sandboxing)
    - Restricted property access via interceptor
    - For performance-critical plugins
    - REQUIRES explicit security audit approval

  Restrictions:
    - Still sandboxed via interceptors
    - Cannot bypass permission checks
    - Resource limits still enforced
    - Audit logging mandatory

  Use Case:
    - Only for critical performance-sensitive paths
    - Only with explicit CTO/Security approval
    - Only for first-party code

Capability-Based Security Model:

Instead of traditional role-based access control, we use fine-grained capabilities.
Each plugin receives a set of capabilities it's allowed to use.

Capability {
  id: string                      // "report.read", "datasource.query"
  version: string                 // "v1", "v2"
  grantedTo: string              // plugin ID
  grantedAt: DateTime
  expiresAt?: DateTime            // Optional expiration
  revokedAt?: DateTime            // If revoked

  permissions: Permission[]       // What this capability allows
  constraints: Constraint[]       // How it's constrained
}

Permission {
  resource: string               // "report", "datasource", "export"
  action: string                // "read", "create", "update", "delete"
  conditions?: {
    scope?: "own_tenant" | "all_tenants"
    resourceIds?: string[]       // Specific resources only
    requiredFlags?: string[]    // Requires user flags
  }
}

Constraint {
  type: "rate-limit" | "resource-limit" | "time-based" | "scope-based"
  value: unknown

  // rate-limit example:
  // {type: "rate-limit", value: {requestsPerSecond: 100}}

  // resource-limit example:
  // {type: "resource-limit", value: {maxMemory: "256MB", maxCpuTime: 30000}}

  // time-based example:
  // {type: "time-based", value: {until: DateTime}}

  // scope-based example:
  // {type: "scope-based", value: {allowedTenants: ["tenant-a", "tenant-b"]}}
}

Capability Grant Process:

  1. Manifest declares: "requires": ["report.read", "datasource.query"]
  2. Registry validates against installed plugins
  3. During load, PluginContext.api constructed with only granted capabilities
  4. Each capability call goes through CapabilityController
  5. Controller checks:
     - Is capability granted?
     - Has it been revoked?
     - Have rate limits been exceeded?
     - Are time-based constraints still valid?
  6. If approved: Execute, log call, track metrics
  7. If denied: Throw PermissionDeniedError with audit log

Host API Surface (Capabilities):

reports.*:
  - read(reportId: string): Promise<Report>
  - create(spec: ReportSpec): Promise<Report>
  - update(id: string, changes: Partial<Report>): Promise<Report>
  - list(filter?: ReportFilter): Promise<Report[]>

datasources.*:
  - query(sourceId: string, sql: string, params?: unknown[]): Promise<QueryResult>
  - listSources(): Promise<DataSource[]>
  - getSchema(sourceId: string): Promise<Schema>

export.*:
  - registerFormat(format: ExportFormat): void
  - transform(data: unknown, format: string): Promise<Buffer>

events.*:
  - on(event: string, handler: Function): () => void     // Returns unsubscribe
  - emit(event: string, data: unknown): void
  - once(event: string): Promise<unknown>

storage.*:
  - getItem(key: string): Promise<unknown>
  - setItem(key: string, value: unknown): Promise<void>
  - deleteItem(key: string): Promise<void>

logger:
  - debug(message: string, context?: any): void
  - info(message: string, context?: any): void
  - warn(message: string, context?: any): void
  - error(message: string, error?: Error, context?: any): void

utils:
  - validate(schema: JSONSchema, data: unknown): boolean
  - uuid(): string
  - delay(ms: number): Promise<void>

Threat Model and Mitigations:

Threat: Data Exfiltration
  - Unauthorized plugin access to sensitive data
  - Plugin sends data to external service
  Mitigations:
    - Network allowlisting (domain/port restrictions)
    - Request inspection for sensitive data
    - Tenant-scoped capabilities (no cross-tenant access)
    - Audit logging of all network calls

Threat: Privilege Escalation
  - Plugin gains elevated permissions
  - Plugin accesses forbidden APIs
  Mitigations:
    - No filesystem access by default
    - No process spawning capability
    - Capabilities are immutable (cannot be modified by plugin)
    - Strict permission checking at API boundary

Threat: Denial of Service
  - Plugin exhausts resources (memory, CPU, network)
  - Plugin crashes host application
  Mitigations:
    - Memory limits enforced by Worker thread heap
    - CPU time budgets with timeout enforcement
    - Network bandwidth metering
    - Connection limits
    - Circuit breakers on cascading failures

Threat: Supply Chain Attack
  - Malicious dependencies in plugin
  - Plugin manifest tampering
  Mitigations:
    - Manifest signature verification
    - Dependency version pinning (no floating versions)
    - Checksum verification of plugin code
    - Dependency vulnerability scanning
    - Security scanning before deployment

Threat: Timing Attacks
  - Plugin exploits timing differences in secure operations
  Mitigations:
    - Constant-time comparisons in sensitive paths
    - Response time normalization
    - Avoid leaking information via timing

Threat: Malicious Dependencies
  - Plugin's npm dependencies compromised
  Mitigations:
    - Lock files (package-lock.json, yarn.lock)
    - Checksum verification before execution
    - Dependency audit tools (npm audit, snyk)
    - Private npm registry with scanning

================================================================================
6. VERSIONED API CONTRACT
================================================================================

Semantic Versioning Strategy:

  MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH-PRERELEASE+BUILD

  MAJOR: Breaking changes, removed capabilities
    - Incompatible with previous version
    - Requires plugin update
    - Example: Changing Report interface structure

  MINOR: New capabilities, backward-compatible additions
    - Works with code written for previous version
    - Plugin can use new features if needed
    - Example: Adding new datasource.queryAsync() method

  PATCH: Bug fixes, performance improvements
    - No breaking changes
    - No new features
    - Transparent update
    - Example: Fixing memory leak in query handler

Version Ranges (npm semver):

  ^2.1.0  = >=2.1.0 <3.0.0   (compatible releases)
  ~2.1.0  = >=2.1.0 <2.2.0   (patch updates only)
  2.1.0   = exactly 2.1.0    (pinned version)
  *       = any version       (not recommended)
  >=2.0.0 = any version >= 2.0.0

API Co-Existence:

  We maintain multiple API versions simultaneously:

  Host Version 1.0 supports:
    - APIv1 (stable)

  Host Version 2.0 supports:
    - APIv1 (with compatibility shim)
    - APIv2 (new stable)

  Host Version 3.0 supports:
    - APIv2 (stable)
    - APIv3 (new stable)
    - APIv1 (deprecated, removed)

  Plugin declares: "apiVersion": ">=v1 <v3"
    - Can use v1 or v2 APIs
    - Cannot use v3 APIs
    - Host provides appropriate version

Example: Deprecated Feature Migration

  v1.0.0: Method reportData() introduced
  v2.0.0: Method reportData() deprecated, queryReport() added
  v2.1.0: Using reportData() logs deprecation warning
  v2.2.0: Migration guide published
  v3.0.0: Method reportData() removed (breaking change)

  Timeline:
    2026-01-01: Deprecated in v2.0.0
    2026-01-01 - 2027-01-01: Supported with warnings
    2027-01-01: Removed in v3.0.0

Deprecation Path:

  interface DeprecationNotice {
    feature: string                          // e.g., "HostAPIv1.reports.read"
    deprecated: string                       // version (e.g., "2.0.0")
    removedIn: string                       // version (e.g., "3.0.0")
    replacement?: string                    // e.g., "HostAPIv2.reports.read"
    alternative?: string                    // e.g., "Use readBatch() for multiple"
    migrationGuide: string                  // URL to guide
    warningLevel: "error" | "warn" | "info"
  }

Deprecation Process:

  1. Announce Deprecation (minimum 3 months notice)
     - Release notes mention feature deprecation
     - Documentation updated with migration guide
     - Release version published

  2. Support Phase (3-12 months)
     - Feature continues to work
     - Log warnings when deprecated API used
     - Provide compatibility shim if possible
     - SDK updated with deprecation notices

  3. Removal (MAJOR version bump)
     - Feature removed from codebase
     - Plugin using old API must update
     - Error returned if old API called

  4. Communication
     - Email notification to plugin developers
     - Blog post on migration steps
     - Office hours/webinars for Q&A

Vendor Lock Prevention:

  To prevent vendor lock-in:

  1. Published API Interfaces
     - All APIs documented publicly
     - TypeScript definitions included
     - OpenAPI specs available

  2. Migration Guides
     - Step-by-step guides for each major version
     - Automated migration tools where possible
     - Side-by-side API comparisons

  3. Backwards Compatibility
     - Support old APIs via shims for as long as feasible
     - Allow plugin to target multiple API versions
     - Provide compatibility layers

  4. Standard Compliance
     - APIs follow industry standards where applicable
     - Use standard formats (JSON, REST, etc.)
     - Avoid proprietary extensions

  Manifest declares:
    "spec": {
      "apiSurfaces": [
        {
          "name": "reports",
          "version": "2.0.0",
          "vendorExtensions": false,
          "standardCompliance": "rest-standard-v1"
        }
      ]
    }

  System validates:
    - No vendor-specific (proprietary) methods used
    - Portability guide provided
    - Plugin can be swapped with compatible alternative

================================================================================
7. CONFIGURATION & STATE MANAGEMENT
================================================================================

Configuration Hierarchy (Each level overrides previous):

  1. Host Defaults
     - Built-in defaults from host application

  2. Environment Variables
     - PLUGIN_<ID>_<KEY> overrides host defaults

  3. Plugin Manifest Defaults
     - spec.configuration.schema.properties[key].default

  4. Tenant-Specific Configuration
     - Per-tenant overrides in configuration store

  5. Runtime Configuration Changes
     - Applied during execution via setConfig()

Configuration Store Interface:

  async getConfig(pluginId: string): Promise<Record<string, unknown>>
    - Retrieve full plugin configuration
    - Includes all levels applied

  async setConfig(
    pluginId: string,
    config: Record<string, unknown>
  ): Promise<void>
    - Update configuration
    - Validates against manifest schema
    - Persists to store
    - Triggers onConfigChange hook if plugin loaded

  async getTenantConfig(
    pluginId: string,
    tenantId: string
  ): Promise<Record<string, unknown>>
    - Get tenant-specific overrides

  watch(
    pluginId: string,
    handler: (config: Record<string, unknown>) => void
  ): () => void
    - Subscribe to configuration changes
    - Returns unsubscribe function

  validate(
    pluginId: string,
    config: Record<string, unknown>
  ): ValidationResult
    - Validate configuration against manifest schema
    - Returns errors and warnings

Plugin State Management:

  interface PluginStore {
    // Single item operations
    get<T>(key: string): Promise<T | undefined>
    set<T>(key: string, value: T, ttl?: number): Promise<void>
    delete(key: string): Promise<void>

    // Batch operations
    getMany(keys: string[]): Promise<Record<string, unknown>>
    setMany(items: Record<string, unknown>, ttl?: number): Promise<void>
    deleteMany(keys: string[]): Promise<void>

    // Scanning
    keys(pattern?: string): Promise<string[]>

    // Expiration
    setExpire(key: string, ttl: number): Promise<void>
    getExpire(key: string): Promise<number | -1>  // -1 = no expiration
  }

State Characteristics:

  - Scoped to plugin + tenant (no cross-tenant leakage)
  - Persisted to durable storage (database, filesystem)
  - TTL support for automatic expiration
  - Transactional updates (atomic)
  - Encrypted for sensitive data
  - Survives plugin unload/reload
  - Queryable by pattern matching

State Storage Layers:

  1. In-Memory Cache (fast, per-host)
     - LRU cache with TTL
     - Warm via lazy loading
     - Invalidated on update

  2. Persistent Store (durable, shared)
     - Database (Redis, Postgres, etc.)
     - Filesystem with fsync
     - Replicated for HA

  3. Recovery (crash resilience)
     - Write-ahead log for transactions
     - Crash recovery on startup
     - Backup snapshots

================================================================================
8. ERROR HANDLING & OBSERVABILITY
================================================================================

Error Classification:

  Manifest & Dependency Errors:
    - MANIFEST_INVALID: Schema validation failed
    - MANIFEST_NOT_FOUND: manifest.json not found
    - DEPENDENCY_UNMET: Required plugin not installed
    - DEPENDENCY_VERSION_CONFLICT: No satisfying version found

  Runtime Errors:
    - TIMEOUT: Operation exceeded time limit
    - OUT_OF_MEMORY: Heap limit exceeded
    - CRASHED: Unhandled exception in sandbox
    - SANDBOX_INIT_FAILED: Failed to initialize sandbox

  Permission Errors:
    - PERMISSION_DENIED: Capability not granted
    - CAPABILITY_UNAVAILABLE: Capability not exported by dependencies

  Configuration Errors:
    - CONFIG_INVALID: Schema validation failed
    - CONFIG_MISSING: Required config field missing

  Lifecycle Errors:
    - LOAD_FAILED: onLoad hook failed
    - UNLOAD_FAILED: onUnload hook failed
    - ENABLE_FAILED: onEnable hook failed
    - DISABLE_FAILED: onDisable hook failed

PluginError Interface:

  interface PluginError extends Error {
    type: PluginErrorType
    pluginId: string
    pluginVersion: string
    timestamp: DateTime
    context: Record<string, unknown>
    recoverable: boolean           // Can plugin recover?
    suggestedAction?: string       // User-facing recovery hint
    correlationId: string          // Trace correlation
    stack: string                  // Full stack trace
  }

Structured Logging:

  Every plugin operation logged with context:

  interface PluginLogEntry {
    timestamp: DateTime
    level: "debug" | "info" | "warn" | "error"

    // Identity
    pluginId: string
    pluginVersion: string

    // Operation
    operation: string              // "load", "query", "export", "event:fire"
    duration: number               // milliseconds
    status: "success" | "failure" | "timeout"

    // Context
    userId?: string
    tenantId?: string
    correlationId: string
    traceId: string

    // Capabilities Used
    capabilities: string[]         // Which APIs were called

    // Resource Usage
    resourceUsage: {
      memoryDelta: number          // Bytes allocated/freed
      cpuTime: number              // Milliseconds
      networkBytes: number         // Bytes sent/received
    }

    // Error Information
    error?: {
      message: string
      stack: string
      type: PluginErrorType
    }

    // Additional Context
    metadata: Record<string, unknown>
  }

Log Levels by Environment:

  Development:
    - DEBUG and above logged
    - All operations logged
    - Full stack traces
    - Performance metrics

  Staging:
    - INFO and above logged
    - High-risk operations logged
    - Error stack traces
    - Performance thresholds

  Production:
    - WARN and above logged
    - Only critical operations
    - Production errors only
    - Performance alerts

Metrics Tracked:

  Per-Plugin Metrics:
    - Load time (P50, P95, P99)
    - Enable time (P50, P95, P99)
    - Memory usage (current, peak, trend)
    - CPU time per operation (P50, P95, P99)
    - Error rate (errors/total operations)
    - Error types (breakdown)
    - API call frequency (by method)
    - Query latency (P50, P95, P99)
    - Event throughput (events/second)
    - Network bandwidth (bytes/second)

  System Metrics:
    - Total plugins loaded
    - Plugins in error state
    - Plugin startup failures
    - Dependency conflicts
    - Security violations
    - Permission denials

Health Monitoring:

  interface HealthCheck {
    enabled: boolean
    interval: number               // milliseconds
    timeout: number
    custom?: (context: PluginContext) => Promise<HealthStatus>
  }

  Automatic health checks:
    1. Every N seconds (configurable)
    2. Call plugin.healthCheck() if defined
    3. Track response time and status
    4. If fails repeatedly, mark degraded
    5. After threshold failures, mark failed
    6. Notify operators

  Operators can:
    - View health status per plugin
    - Configure check intervals
    - Set alert thresholds
    - Manually trigger checks

================================================================================
9. TESTING & VALIDATION
================================================================================

Plugin Validation Checklist:

  Every plugin must pass:

  Manifest Validation:
    - manifestValid: Schema validation passes
    - manifestErrors: List of schema violations

  Dependency Validation:
    - dependenciesResolvable: All dependencies can be resolved
    - dependencyErrors: List of missing/conflicting deps

  Code Quality:
    - noUnsafeGlobals: No access to process, os, fs directly
    - noDirectFileAccess: No require('fs') or equivalent
    - noProcessSpawning: No child process spawning
    - noEval: No eval(), Function() constructor

  Security:
    - noHardcodedSecrets: Scanned for API keys, passwords
    - noSuspiciousImports: No suspicious npm packages
    - networkCallsAllowlisted: All domains in allowedDomains
    - permissionsMinimal: Only requests needed capabilities

  Performance:
    - loadTimeUnder5s: Plugin loads in < 5 seconds
    - initialMemoryUnder100mb: Uses < 100MB on startup
    - enableTimeUnder2s: onEnable completes < 2 seconds

  Compatibility:
    - apiVersionCompatible: Plugin's apiVersion >= host's
    - hostVersionCompatible: Plugin's hostVersion matches

Test Harness Interface:

  interface PluginTestHarness {
    // Initialization
    createSandbox(): Promise<TestSandbox>

    // Mocking
    mockAPI(api: Partial<HostAPI>): void

    // Lifecycle
    load(config?: Record<string, unknown>): Promise<void>
    enable(): Promise<void>
    disable(): Promise<void>
    unload(): Promise<void>

    // Capability Invocation
    call<T>(capability: string, params: unknown): Promise<T>

    // State Access
    getMetrics(): PluginMetrics
    getConfig(): Record<string, unknown>
    getState(key: string): Promise<unknown>

    // Error Injection
    throwError(error: Error): void
    timeout(operation: string): void

    // Clock Advancement
    advanceClock(ms: number): void

    // Assertions
    assert: {
      eventEmitted(pattern: string): boolean
      apiCalled(method: string, args?: any[]): boolean
      resourceLimitExceeded(resource: string): boolean
    }

    // Cleanup
    cleanup(): Promise<void>
  }

Test Harness Usage:

  import {PluginTestHarness} from '@reporting-tool/plugin-testing'

  describe('Report Exporter Plugin', () => {
    let harness: PluginTestHarness

    beforeEach(async () => {
      harness = new PluginTestHarness('report-exporter')

      // Mock host APIs
      harness.mockAPI({
        reports: {
          read: async (id) => ({id, title: 'Test Report'})
        }
      })
    })

    it('should handle network timeout gracefully', async () => {
      await harness.load({apiKey: 'test-key'})
      await harness.enable()

      harness.throwError(new TimeoutError('Network timeout'))

      const result = await harness.call('report.export.pdf', {reportId: '1'})

      expect(harness.assert.eventEmitted('error:*')).toBe(true)
      expect(harness.getMetrics().errorCount).toBeGreaterThan(0)
    })

    it('should respect memory limits', async () => {
      await harness.load()

      // Allocate memory until limit
      while (harness.getMetrics().memoryUsage < 250 * 1024 * 1024) {
        // Large allocation
      }

      expect(harness.assert.resourceLimitExceeded('memory')).toBe(true)
    })

    afterEach(async () => {
      await harness.cleanup()
    })
  })

Security Scanning:

  Pre-Deployment Security Checks:

  1. Static Analysis
     - AST scanning for unsafe patterns
     - Forbidden API detection
     - Code complexity analysis

  2. Secret Scanning
     - Regex patterns: /password|api.?key|secret|token/i
     - Entropy detection
     - Common secret formats (base64, hex)

  3. Unsafe API Calls
     - require('fs'), require('child_process')
     - process.exit(), eval()
     - process.env access

  4. Dependency Audit
     - npm audit for vulnerabilities
     - Check for outdated packages
     - License compliance

  5. Network Analysis
     - Extract all network calls
     - Validate against allowedDomains
     - Check for hardcoded IPs/URLs

  6. Permission Analysis
     - Extract declared permissions
     - Identify unused capabilities
     - Check for permission elevation

  Scan Result:

  interface SecurityScan {
    staticAnalysis: {
      suspiciousPatterns: Finding[]
      hardcodedSecrets: Finding[]
      unsafeAPICalls: Finding[]
    }
    dependencyAudit: {
      vulnerabilities: Vulnerability[]
      outdatedPackages: Package[]
      licenseIssues: string[]
    }
    networkAnalysis: {
      externalCallsDetected: NetworkCall[]
      unapprovedDomains: string[]
    }
    permissionAnalysis: {
      requestedCapabilities: string[]
      unusedCapabilities: string[]
    }
    verdict: "approved" | "approved_with_warnings" | "rejected"
    summary: string
  }

