13. DESIGN DECISIONS & TRADEOFFS
================================================================================

Decision: Worker Threads vs Processes

  CHOSEN: Worker Threads (with Process as opt-in)

  Advantages of Worker Threads:
    + Lower overhead (minimal extra memory)
    + Faster IPC (vs process communication)
    + Simpler debugging
    + Shared host resources efficiently

  Disadvantages:
    - Single V8 crashes crash host (mitigated by error handling)
    - Less isolation than processes

  Advantages of Processes:
    + Complete isolation
    + OS-level security boundaries
    + Can restart independently

  Disadvantages:
    - Much higher memory overhead
    - Slower IPC (spawning, serialization)
    - More complex debugging

  Decision: Use Worker Threads by default, Process isolation available for
  high-risk plugins via sandboxLevel: "permissive"

Decision: Capability-Based vs Role-Based Security

  CHOSEN: Capability-Based (fine-grained permissions)

  Advantages:
    + Principle of least privilege
    + No privilege escalation vector
    + Fine-grained audit trail
    + Flexible revocation

  Disadvantages:
    - More complex to implement
    - Requires explicit permission requests

  Role-Based would provide:
    + Simpler management ("Admin", "Editor", "Viewer")
    - But higher risk of over-permissions
    - Less audit flexibility

  Decision: Use capability-based model for stronger security posture

Decision: Manifest Validation Timing

  CHOSEN: Strict schema validation at load time

  Advantages:
    + Catches errors early
    + Fails fast before execution
    + Consistent error handling

  Disadvantages:
    - Longer startup time
    - Stricter requirements on plugins

  Lazy validation alternative:
    + Faster startup
    - Errors discovered during execution
    - Harder to debug

  Decision: Validate at load time (production systems prefer reliability)

Decision: Error Recovery Strategy

  CHOSEN: Exponential backoff with automatic quarantine

  Strategy:
    - Transient errors: retry with backoff
    - Permanent errors: fail immediately
    - After 3 failures: quarantine
    - Manual intervention required for quarantine

  Advantages:
    + Handles transient failures
    + Prevents cascade failures
    + Operator control

  Disadvantages:
    - Some recovery attempts during backoff
    - Operator intervention required for manual recovery

  Alternative (always retry):
    + Never gives up
    - Can cause cascading failures
    - Unbounded retry loops

  Decision: Exponential backoff with quarantine (production safety)

Decision: State Management

  CHOSEN: Distributed eventual consistency

  Approach:
    - Each plugin has local state
    - Optional central state store
    - Write-through to persistent storage
    - Eventual consistency between layers

  Advantages:
    + Prevents deadlocks
    + Scales horizontally
    + Good performance

  Disadvantages:
    - Possible temporary inconsistency
    - Requires idempotency

  Strongly consistent alternative:
    + Guaranteed consistency
    - More locking overhead
    - Potential deadlocks
    - Harder to scale

  Decision: Eventual consistency (distributed systems best practices)

Decision: API Versioning

  CHOSEN: Semantic versioning with backwards compatibility shims

  Approach:
    - MAJOR: Breaking changes
    - MINOR: New features
    - PATCH: Bugfixes
    - Multiple versions supported simultaneously
    - Compatibility adapters for old versions

  Advantages:
    + Clear version semantics
    + Prevents vendor lock-in
    + Gradual migration paths

  Disadvantages:
    - Maintenance burden for compatibility
    - Complexity in implementation

  Alternative (always breaking):
    + Simpler implementation
    - Forces all plugins to update
    - Risky during critical updates

  Decision: Semantic versioning with bridges (prevent lock-in)

