================================================================================
LIFECYCLE MANAGEMENT
================================================================================

State Machine:
  UNINSTALLED -> install() -> INSTALLED -> load() -> LOADING -> LOADED
                                                        |
                                              enable() |-> ENABLING -> ENABLED
                                                        |   disable() ^
                                                        |      |      |
                                                        |      v      |
                                                        +-- DISABLED -+
                                                        |
                                              unload() |-> UNLOADING -> UNINSTALLED

Lifecycle Hooks (with timeouts):
  - onLoad(): Initialize plugin
  - onEnable(): Activate plugin  
  - onDisable(): Suspend plugin
  - onUnload(): Cleanup plugin
  - onError(): Error handling

Hook Properties:
  - timeout: Must complete within ms
  - critical: If true, failure marks plugin ERROR
  - Sequential execution (dependent plugins wait)
  - Timeout/exception triggers rollback

PluginContext (available in hooks):
  - pluginId, version, config
  - logger, store, emit
  - api (controlled host access)
  - metrics, tracing

================================================================================
SANDBOXED EXECUTION
================================================================================

Three Isolation Levels:

STRICT (Default - Most Secure):
  - Worker Thread with separate V8 isolate
  - Message passing only
  - Memory: 256MB limit
  - Network: Explicit allowlist
  - Filesystem: No access
  - No process spawning

MODERATE (Medium Security):
  - Worker Thread
  - Filesystem read access allowed
  - Network less restricted
  - Memory: 512MB limit
  - For trusted vendors

PERMISSIVE (Minimum Security):
  - Same-process execution
  - For performance-critical only
  - REQUIRES security audit approval

Capability-Based Security:

Instead of roles, plugins get capabilities:

Capability:
  - id: "report.read", "datasource.query"
  - version: "v1"
  - grantedTo: plugin ID
  - permissions: What's allowed
  - constraints: Rate limits, resource limits

Permission:
  - resource: "report", "datasource", "export"
  - action: "read", "create", "update", "delete"
  - conditions: scope-based, resource-based

Constraint:
  - type: rate-limit, resource-limit, time-based
  - value: Specific constraint

Enforcement:
  - Every call checked by PermissionManager
  - Denied calls throw PermissionDeniedError
  - All calls logged and monitored

Host API Surface:
  - reports.*: read, create, update, list
  - datasources.*: query, listSources, getSchema
  - export.*: registerFormat, transform
  - events.*: on, emit, once
  - storage.*: getItem, setItem, deleteItem
  - logger: Structured logging
  - utils: validate, uuid, delay

Threat Mitigations:
  - Data Exfiltration: Network allowlist, tenant-scoped capabilities
  - Privilege Escalation: No filesystem/process access
  - DoS: Memory/CPU/network limits, timeouts
  - Supply Chain: Signatures, pinning, scanning
  - Timing Attacks: Constant-time comparisons

================================================================================
VERSIONED API CONTRACT
================================================================================

Semantic Versioning:
  MAJOR: Breaking changes
  MINOR: New features (backward-compatible)
  PATCH: Bug fixes

Version Ranges (npm semver):
  ^2.1.0 = >=2.1.0 <3.0.0
  ~2.1.0 = >=2.1.0 <2.2.0
  2.1.0 = exactly 2.1.0

API Co-Existence:
  - Multiple API versions supported simultaneously
  - Plugins request version range: ">=v1 <v3"
  - Host provides compatible version
  - Backwards compatibility shims maintained

Deprecation Path:
  1. Announce 3+ months before removal
  2. Log warnings when deprecated API used
  3. Support with compatibility shim
  4. Remove in next MAJOR version

Vendor Lock Prevention:
  - Published API interfaces
  - Migration guides per version
  - Backwards compatibility
  - Standard compliance
  - Portability documentation

